    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Returns to the Annual SEMA Show Featuring a 300-HP Sentra Spec-V Revival Concept, Two Z Cars\nPolaris RZR Pro R Factory Boost Will Thunder Through the Desert with 400 HP and a $200K Sticker\n13 photos\nPhoto: Polaris\nNext year's desert racing season could look a bit different than it used to be if American powersports company Polaris has its way, and the new variant of the RZR side-by-side snatches the minds and pockets of racers.\nCalled the RZR Pro R Factory Boost, Polaris' newest insanity brings together the enhancements announced earlier in the summer for the RZR Pro R Boost and the RZR Pro R Factory, and what resulted is simply mind-boggling.\nBuilt on a reinforced chassis, the Factory Boost throws in a wheelbase that's 6.5 inches (16.5 cm) longer than before, now at 111 inches (282 cm). It is now fitted with larger tubes across structural members, so that it can take a beating in the open desert races it will most definitely be part of soon.\nBecause the side-by-side has been imagined with competitions in mind, its ProStar Fury 2.0-liter engine is not to be taken lightly. Tuned for racing, the four-cylinder cranks out 400 horsepower. That's the same as the RZR Pro R Factory it will soon join, but a lot more than this summer's Boost model, which is only good for 275 horsepower.\nThe engine draws fuel from a 45-gallon (170-liter) fuel cell, larger than on the base model (which stands at 34 gallons/128 liters) and good for as many as 200 miles (322 km). All of that, as well as an all-new cage, is hidden under a limited carbon fiber bodywork that wraps around a slightly wider cabin –two inches (five cm).\nFor suspension, the Polaris RZR Pro R Factory Boost uses three-inch (7.6 cm) internal bypass Dynamix DV shocks that have been modified with race-specific components, while a standard Fox steering damper makes sure steering and control are just right.\nFinally, Polaris throws in on its new desert racer a fully integrated MoTeC electronics package that comprises, among other things, an upgraded ECU, dual power distribution modules, and a second PDM.\nThe company presented its new vehicle during the annual Camp RZR event at Glamis Imperial Sand Dunes in California, which took place this past weekend. From there, the new vehicle went straight to Polaris' configurator, where it is now listed as available.\nThere will be a limited window for people to order their new desert racer, which closes in mid-February 2026. Polaris is asking $200,000 for it, but that's only the minimum, as speccing it differently from stock will cost more.\nFor the record, the Polaris RZR Pro R Boost that was shown this summer came with an asking price of only $49,999.\nBefore it gets into customer hands, Polaris will take the Pro R Factory Boost out for a spin in the hands of its own factory team during the 2027 King of the Hammers event that takes place in mid-January in California.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPolaris polaris rzr Polaris RZR Pro R Factory Boost\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT All-New 2027 Nissan Rogue Hybrid Just Went on Sale in the US and Already Got a CGI Midnight Edition",
